window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"lessons": [{"id": "lesson1", "number": 1, "title": "Portal landscape and your scope", "subtitle": "Build a mental model before operating any screen.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">1</div><div><h2>Portal landscape and your scope</h2><p>Build a mental model before operating any screen.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Locate Admission within the Campus Information System (CIS) and AAS.</li><li>Distinguish public applicant activity, staff administration, and student intake.</li><li>Describe the limits of an application-operations role.</li></ul></div>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>The public website contains the admission application; the staff Admin Portal contains AAS Admission. They share a backend, server-side session model, and Oracle data. The completion report lists online application/results and student ID creation as Admission capabilities. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">M §1.1</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-102-end-to-end-ownership-matrix\">M §10.2</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=6\">P p. 6</a></p>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">System map · documented components, interpreted handoff</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Applicant</strong><small>Account, application, personal and education details, programme choice, attachments</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>Public website</strong><small>Admission application and result-facing surface</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">↔</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>Backend + Oracle</strong><small>Session, business rules, admission records and files</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">↔</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Staff Admin Portal</strong><small>Application Management and Acceptance Management</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Approved intake</strong><small>New-student creation, then downstream student services</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">The boxes are documented surfaces and the manual’s conceptual flow. The exact action that triggers new-student creation is not specified. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Actor</th><th>Admission-related responsibility</th><th>Authority boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Applicant</td><td>Supplies application data and attachments through the public surface.</td><td>Must meet the configured intake requirements; exact field list is not in M §10.3.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admissions / institutional owner</td><td>Owns screening policy, decision correction, and approval for persistent business changes.</td><td>Named approver and approval route must be confirmed. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Application Operations and Service Coordination</td><td>Verify data and access, triage user requests, keep evidence, coordinate with owners and technical support.</td><td>The role description supports first-line response; it does not grant independent admissions decision authority. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/IT%20Application%20System%20Operator.docx\">R</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Application / infrastructure support</td><td>Investigate application defects, integration, storage, session, and server failures.</td><td>Contacts, service levels, and access rules belong in the protected operations record. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-122-access-request-and-escalation\">M §12.2</a></td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · one intake, three surfaces</h4><p>Fictional applicant <strong>Ama Mensah</strong> applies to Computer Science for Academic Year 2027, Semester 1. Ama uses the public application. Staff use Admission in the Admin Portal to prepare the intake, review her submitted record, and manage a decision. An approved outcome may lead to a student record and later student-portal access. Do not assume that clicking a decision button immediately creates the student ID; the manual does not give that trigger. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Which portal does the applicant use, and which does staff use?</summary><p>The applicant uses the public website’s admission application. Staff use the AAS Admission area of the Admin Portal. Both depend on the shared backend and data. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">M §1.1</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Admission is a cross-surface process. Keep the applicant-facing journey, staff actions, and approved student intake distinct.</div>\n    ", "search": "1Portal landscape and your scopeBuild a mental model before operating any screen. Learning objectivesLocate Admission within the Campus Information System (CIS) and AAS.Distinguish public applicant activity, staff administration, and student intake.Describe the limits of an application-operations role. DOCUMENTEDThe public website contains the admission application; the staff Admin Portal contains AAS Admission. They share a backend, server-side session model, and Oracle data. The completion report lists online application/results and student ID creation as Admission capabilities. M §1.1 M §10.2 P p. 6 System map · documented components, interpreted handoffApplicantAccount, application, personal and education details, programme choice, attachments→Public websiteAdmission application and result-facing surface↔Backend + OracleSession, business rules, admission records and files↔Staff Admin PortalApplication Management and Acceptance Management→Approved intakeNew-student creation, then downstream student services The boxes are documented surfaces and the manual’s conceptual flow. The exact action that triggers new-student creation is not specified. M §10.3 ActorAdmission-related responsibilityAuthority boundary ApplicantSupplies application data and attachments through the public surface.Must meet the configured intake requirements; exact field list is not in M §10.3. Admissions / institutional ownerOwns screening policy, decision correction, and approval for persistent business changes.Named approver and approval route must be confirmed. M §10.11 Application Operations and Service CoordinationVerify data and access, triage user requests, keep evidence, coordinate with owners and technical support.The role description supports first-line response; it does not grant independent admissions decision authority. R Application / infrastructure supportInvestigate application defects, integration, storage, session, and server failures.Contacts, service levels, and access rules belong in the protected operations record. M §12.2 Worked example · one intake, three surfacesFictional applicant Ama Mensah applies to Computer Science for Academic Year 2027, Semester 1. Ama uses the public application. Staff use Admission in the Admin Portal to prepare the intake, review her submitted record, and manage a decision. An approved outcome may lead to a student record and later student-portal access. Do not assume that clicking a decision button immediately creates the student ID; the manual does not give that trigger. M §10.3 Knowledge check · Which portal does the applicant use, and which does staff use?The applicant uses the public website’s admission application. Staff use the AAS Admission area of the Admin Portal. Both depend on the shared backend and data. M §1.1 Key takeaway: Admission is a cross-surface process. Keep the applicant-facing journey, staff actions, and approved student intake distinct."}, {"id": "lesson2", "number": 2, "title": "Access, navigation, fields, and screen meaning", "subtitle": "Read what the interface shows without assuming hidden rules.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">2</div><div><h2>Access, navigation, fields, and screen meaning</h2><p>Read what the interface shows without assuming hidden rules.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Locate the two staff menu groups and six screens.</li><li>Explain the repeated filters and visible buttons.</li><li>Recognise access and session behaviour that can look like a portal fault.</li></ul></div>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>Staff sign in through the Admin login with an authorised non-student role. Admin menu visibility is driven by authorised menus, but backend operations still require permission checks. Both portals use cookies and a server-side session, with a 30-minute inactivity timeout and a warning five minutes before expiry. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-16-authentication-and-authorization\">M §1.6</a></p>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Staff navigation</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Admission</strong><small>AAS module in the Admin Portal</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Application Management</strong><small>Schedule · Criteria · Support Form</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">+</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Acceptance Management</strong><small>Submission · Decision · Report</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">This menu grouping is visible in the supplied staff screenshots. The manual confirms Admission is an AAS domain. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-19-main-domains\">M §1.9</a></p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Screen</th><th>Visible purpose and controls</th><th>Operational reading</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Schedule Management</td><td>Period Search: Year, Semester, Start Date, End Date. Dates display YYYY-MM-DD.</td><td>Find the intended application period before answering timing questions. Screenshot does not show the result list or save controls.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admission Criteria Settings</td><td>Academic Year, Semester, School, Programme; School list includes Agriculture College and Engineering College.</td><td>Scope criteria to the correct intake. Actual requirement values and approval controls are not pictured.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Register Support Form</td><td>Academic Year, Semester, School, Programme. Subtitle says programme information and application form files.</td><td>Locate the correct programme materials; file controls and file contents are not pictured.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Application Submission Management</td><td>Academic Year, Semester, School, Programme, Examination Type. Visible types: WASSCE (Ghanaian), WASSCE (International), SSSCE.</td><td>Filter submitted records for review and aggregate comparison. The review grid itself is not pictured.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admission Decision Management</td><td>Academic Year, Semester, Programme, Examination Type; another crop shows School, Result displaying “Pass”, Search, Cancel.</td><td>Find matched applications and decision records. “Pass” is a visible filter value; its exact business meaning is not documented.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admission Report</td><td>Academic Year, Semester, School, Programme, Search, Cancel.</td><td>Retrieve highlights, applicant statistics, and programme-level results for a matching intake.</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>In manual terminology, School is the parent university unit, Programme is the department, Academic Year is YEAR, and Semester is TM (1 or 2). <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-03-31-terminology\">M §3.1</a> <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span>“All” is visible on School/Programme filters; select a specific value when checking one intake.</p>\n      <p><span class=\"tag interp\">INTERPRETATION</span>Use the same year, semester, school, and programme across related screens when reconciling an intake. Search may apply filters only when pressed on some screens: the developer guide describes that pattern, but its use on each admissions screen must be verified. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-04-43-search-page-pattern\">M §4.3</a></p>\n      <figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image1.png\" alt=\"Schedule Management screen showing the Admission menu and Period Search fields\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 1 · Schedule Management shows the menu grouping and the date, year, and semester fields. Cropped to the visible form.</figcaption></figure>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · locate a cohort</h4><p>You are asked about 2027 Semester 1 applications. Open Admission → Application Management → Schedule Management, select 2027 and Semester 1, and inspect the matching period if displayed. To discuss its entry requirements, move to Admission Criteria Settings and use the same academic year and semester, then narrow to the relevant school and programme. Treat the exact dates and requirements as values to verify, not as values supplied by this guide.</p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Does a hidden menu prove a user is authorised or unauthorised for a backend action?</summary><p>No. Menu visibility governs navigation, while sensitive backend actions require their own authorisation checks. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-16-authentication-and-authorization\">M §1.6</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-03-33-menu-id-and-display-rules\">M §3.3</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> A screenshot identifies visible controls. It does not prove the saved data, permitted actions, or result of pressing a button.</div>\n    ", "search": "2Access, navigation, fields, and screen meaningRead what the interface shows without assuming hidden rules. Learning objectivesLocate the two staff menu groups and six screens.Explain the repeated filters and visible buttons.Recognise access and session behaviour that can look like a portal fault. DOCUMENTEDStaff sign in through the Admin login with an authorised non-student role. Admin menu visibility is driven by authorised menus, but backend operations still require permission checks. Both portals use cookies and a server-side session, with a 30-minute inactivity timeout and a warning five minutes before expiry. M §1.6 Staff navigationAdmissionAAS module in the Admin Portal→Application ManagementSchedule · Criteria · Support Form+Acceptance ManagementSubmission · Decision · Report This menu grouping is visible in the supplied staff screenshots. The manual confirms Admission is an AAS domain. M §1.9 ScreenVisible purpose and controlsOperational reading Schedule ManagementPeriod Search: Year, Semester, Start Date, End Date. Dates display YYYY-MM-DD.Find the intended application period before answering timing questions. Screenshot does not show the result list or save controls. Admission Criteria SettingsAcademic Year, Semester, School, Programme; School list includes Agriculture College and Engineering College.Scope criteria to the correct intake. Actual requirement values and approval controls are not pictured. Register Support FormAcademic Year, Semester, School, Programme. Subtitle says programme information and application form files.Locate the correct programme materials; file controls and file contents are not pictured. Application Submission ManagementAcademic Year, Semester, School, Programme, Examination Type. Visible types: WASSCE (Ghanaian), WASSCE (International), SSSCE.Filter submitted records for review and aggregate comparison. The review grid itself is not pictured. Admission Decision ManagementAcademic Year, Semester, Programme, Examination Type; another crop shows School, Result displaying “Pass”, Search, Cancel.Find matched applications and decision records. “Pass” is a visible filter value; its exact business meaning is not documented. Admission ReportAcademic Year, Semester, School, Programme, Search, Cancel.Retrieve highlights, applicant statistics, and programme-level results for a matching intake. DOCUMENTEDIn manual terminology, School is the parent university unit, Programme is the department, Academic Year is YEAR, and Semester is TM (1 or 2). M §3.1 SCREENSHOT“All” is visible on School/Programme filters; select a specific value when checking one intake. INTERPRETATIONUse the same year, semester, school, and programme across related screens when reconciling an intake. Search may apply filters only when pressed on some screens: the developer guide describes that pattern, but its use on each admissions screen must be verified. M §4.3 UI figure 1 · Schedule Management shows the menu grouping and the date, year, and semester fields. Cropped to the visible form. Worked example · locate a cohortYou are asked about 2027 Semester 1 applications. Open Admission → Application Management → Schedule Management, select 2027 and Semester 1, and inspect the matching period if displayed. To discuss its entry requirements, move to Admission Criteria Settings and use the same academic year and semester, then narrow to the relevant school and programme. Treat the exact dates and requirements as values to verify, not as values supplied by this guide. Knowledge check · Does a hidden menu prove a user is authorised or unauthorised for a backend action?No. Menu visibility governs navigation, while sensitive backend actions require their own authorisation checks. M §1.6 M §3.3 Key takeaway: A screenshot identifies visible controls. It does not prove the saved data, permitted actions, or result of pressing a button."}, {"id": "lesson3", "number": 3, "title": "Prepare an admission intake", "subtitle": "Align opening dates, requirements, and application materials.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">3</div><div><h2>Prepare an admission intake</h2><p>Align opening dates, requirements, and application materials.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Explain the three Application Management tabs as one preparation sequence.</li><li>Identify prerequisites, inputs, decision owners, outputs, and exceptions.</li><li>Apply a pre-opening check without inventing a publication button.</li></ul></div>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Pre-opening sequence · recommended operating order</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Choose intake</strong><small>Academic year, semester, school, programme</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Schedule</strong><small>Confirm application period</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Criteria</strong><small>Confirm requirements and attachments</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Support Form</strong><small>Confirm programme information and form file</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Owner check</strong><small>Approve readiness before opening</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">The three screens and their purposes are visible in UI figures 1–4. The final owner check is a recommendation; the sources do not specify a formal approval step or publication mechanism. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Prerequisite and input</th><th>Operator check</th><th>Output / exception</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>1 · Schedule</td><td>Approved intake identity and application dates.</td><td>Search by year, semester, and date range; verify the period belongs to the intended intake.</td><td>Period is consistent. If missing or conflicting, ask the schedule owner; do not announce unverified dates.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>2 · Criteria</td><td>Selected School/Programme must exist and be open; institutional criteria must be approved. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td><td>Select year, semester, school, programme; compare requirement and attachment rules with approved policy.</td><td>Correct criteria for the cohort. If policy is absent or contradictory, stop a persistent rule change and obtain written approval. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>3 · Support Form</td><td>Approved programme information and correct application form file.</td><td>Use the same cohort filters; verify that the current materials are associated with it.</td><td>Materials are ready. If missing or wrong, involve the content owner and authorised file operator; attachment storage controls matter. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-129-file-storage-and-retention\">M §12.9</a></td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"screenpair\"><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image2.png\" alt=\"Admission Criteria Settings screen with academic year, semester, and school fields\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 2 · Criteria Settings: year, semester, school.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image3.png\" alt=\"Programme dropdown with example programme names\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 3 · Programme options include Computer Science and Agricultural Technology.</figcaption></figure></div>\n      <figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image4.png\" alt=\"Register Support Form screen with year, semester, school, and programme filters\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 4 · Register Support Form: programme information and application form files are named in the page subtitle.</figcaption></figure>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · 2027 Semester 2 Computer Science</h4><p>First confirm the 2027 Semester 2 application period. Then select that year and semester, Engineering College (if that remains Computer Science’s current school), and Computer Science in Criteria Settings. Compare the displayed criteria with the Admissions office’s approved version. In Register Support Form, use the same cohort and inspect the programme information and form file. Record any mismatch and who approved the correction. The example does not prescribe specific dates or entry grades because the sources provide none.</p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Why should you not use “All” when investigating one programme’s missing form?</summary><p>“All” broadens the result set. Selecting the exact year, semester, school, and programme reduces the risk of inspecting or updating a different intake. The specific filters are visible in the screenshots.</p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Prepare the three parts against one cohort identity and an approved source of policy before applications open.</div>\n    ", "search": "3Prepare an admission intakeAlign opening dates, requirements, and application materials. Learning objectivesExplain the three Application Management tabs as one preparation sequence.Identify prerequisites, inputs, decision owners, outputs, and exceptions.Apply a pre-opening check without inventing a publication button. Pre-opening sequence · recommended operating orderChoose intakeAcademic year, semester, school, programme→ScheduleConfirm application period→CriteriaConfirm requirements and attachments→Support FormConfirm programme information and form file→Owner checkApprove readiness before opening The three screens and their purposes are visible in UI figures 1–4. The final owner check is a recommendation; the sources do not specify a formal approval step or publication mechanism. M §10.3 StepPrerequisite and inputOperator checkOutput / exception 1 · ScheduleApproved intake identity and application dates.Search by year, semester, and date range; verify the period belongs to the intended intake.Period is consistent. If missing or conflicting, ask the schedule owner; do not announce unverified dates. 2 · CriteriaSelected School/Programme must exist and be open; institutional criteria must be approved. M §10.3Select year, semester, school, programme; compare requirement and attachment rules with approved policy.Correct criteria for the cohort. If policy is absent or contradictory, stop a persistent rule change and obtain written approval. M §10.11 3 · Support FormApproved programme information and correct application form file.Use the same cohort filters; verify that the current materials are associated with it.Materials are ready. If missing or wrong, involve the content owner and authorised file operator; attachment storage controls matter. M §12.9 UI figure 2 · Criteria Settings: year, semester, school.UI figure 3 · Programme options include Computer Science and Agricultural Technology. UI figure 4 · Register Support Form: programme information and application form files are named in the page subtitle. Worked example · 2027 Semester 2 Computer ScienceFirst confirm the 2027 Semester 2 application period. Then select that year and semester, Engineering College (if that remains Computer Science’s current school), and Computer Science in Criteria Settings. Compare the displayed criteria with the Admissions office’s approved version. In Register Support Form, use the same cohort and inspect the programme information and form file. Record any mismatch and who approved the correction. The example does not prescribe specific dates or entry grades because the sources provide none. Knowledge check · Why should you not use “All” when investigating one programme’s missing form?“All” broadens the result set. Selecting the exact year, semester, school, and programme reduces the risk of inspecting or updating a different intake. The specific filters are visible in the screenshots. Key takeaway: Prepare the three parts against one cohort identity and an approved source of policy before applications open."}, {"id": "lesson4", "number": 4, "title": "Receive applications and review submissions", "subtitle": "Follow the documented conceptual flow into staff screening.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">4</div><div><h2>Receive applications and review submissions</h2><p>Follow the documented conceptual flow into staff screening.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Trace applicant data from the public surface into the staff review area.</li><li>Use the visible submission filters and examination types.</li><li>Distinguish a review record from a final admission decision.</li></ul></div>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>The manual describes an applicant account/application followed by personal, education, and programme data plus attachments, then administrative screening/selection. Representative backend endpoints cover applicant selection/list/detail, forms, uploads, and collective processing. It does not supply a field-by-field public form or a complete click sequence. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Submission review · conceptual flow</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Applicant submits</strong><small>Account, data, programme choice, attachments</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>System records</strong><small>Application and file data; exact validation rules need confirmation</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Staff filter</strong><small>Year, semester, school, programme, examination type</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Review</strong><small>Check submitted evidence and compare aggregates</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>Review record</strong><small>Pass forward for decision handling</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">The staff page subtitle supports review of submitted applications, examination aggregates, and review records. “Pass forward” is an interpreted workflow relationship, not a verified button or automatic status transition.</p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Review element</th><th>What is supported</th><th>What remains to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Filters</td><td>2027 and Semester 1 are shown; School and Programme can be broad or specific; Examination Type offers WASSCE (Ghanaian), WASSCE (International), and SSSCE. <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span></td><td>Whether other exam categories exist in the current deployment and whether filters are applied automatically or through a Search button.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Aggregate comparison</td><td>Named in the page subtitle. <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span></td><td>The calculation, pass threshold, evidence sources, and override policy.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Attachments</td><td>The manual includes applicant attachments and admission files. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td><td>Required file list, size/type limits, retention, reviewer access, and how a missing file is corrected.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Review record</td><td>The page subtitle says staff manage review records. <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span></td><td>Exact review statuses, assignment, audit fields, and whether review is separate from a decision approval.</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"screenpair\"><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image6.png\" alt=\"Application Submission Management page with year, semester, and school filters\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 5 · Submission Management purpose and first three filters.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image7.png\" alt=\"Submission Management programme and examination type filters\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 6 · Programme and examination-type choices.</figcaption></figure></div>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · fictional WASSCE applicant</h4><p><strong>Ama Mensah</strong>, reference <strong>TEST-ADM-027</strong>, applied for Computer Science in 2027 Semester 1 using Ghanaian WASSCE results. Filter Submission Management to that year, semester, school, programme, and WASSCE (Ghanaian). Compare the displayed aggregate and attachments to the approved criteria. If a document is absent, record the missing evidence and ask the Admissions owner which correction route is permitted; do not invent a “rejected” status or overwrite the file yourself.</p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Is comparing an examination aggregate the same as approving admission?</summary><p>No. The screenshot identifies aggregate comparison in submission review. The manual separates administrative screening/selection from the admission result, and the interface has a separate Decision Management page. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Review submitted evidence first; treat final result handling as a separate controlled step.</div>\n    ", "search": "4Receive applications and review submissionsFollow the documented conceptual flow into staff screening. Learning objectivesTrace applicant data from the public surface into the staff review area.Use the visible submission filters and examination types.Distinguish a review record from a final admission decision. DOCUMENTEDThe manual describes an applicant account/application followed by personal, education, and programme data plus attachments, then administrative screening/selection. Representative backend endpoints cover applicant selection/list/detail, forms, uploads, and collective processing. It does not supply a field-by-field public form or a complete click sequence. M §10.3 Submission review · conceptual flowApplicant submitsAccount, data, programme choice, attachments→System recordsApplication and file data; exact validation rules need confirmation→Staff filterYear, semester, school, programme, examination type→ReviewCheck submitted evidence and compare aggregates→Review recordPass forward for decision handling The staff page subtitle supports review of submitted applications, examination aggregates, and review records. “Pass forward” is an interpreted workflow relationship, not a verified button or automatic status transition. Review elementWhat is supportedWhat remains to verify Filters2027 and Semester 1 are shown; School and Programme can be broad or specific; Examination Type offers WASSCE (Ghanaian), WASSCE (International), and SSSCE. SCREENSHOTWhether other exam categories exist in the current deployment and whether filters are applied automatically or through a Search button. Aggregate comparisonNamed in the page subtitle. SCREENSHOTThe calculation, pass threshold, evidence sources, and override policy. AttachmentsThe manual includes applicant attachments and admission files. M §10.3Required file list, size/type limits, retention, reviewer access, and how a missing file is corrected. Review recordThe page subtitle says staff manage review records. SCREENSHOTExact review statuses, assignment, audit fields, and whether review is separate from a decision approval. UI figure 5 · Submission Management purpose and first three filters.UI figure 6 · Programme and examination-type choices. Worked example · fictional WASSCE applicantAma Mensah, reference TEST-ADM-027, applied for Computer Science in 2027 Semester 1 using Ghanaian WASSCE results. Filter Submission Management to that year, semester, school, programme, and WASSCE (Ghanaian). Compare the displayed aggregate and attachments to the approved criteria. If a document is absent, record the missing evidence and ask the Admissions owner which correction route is permitted; do not invent a “rejected” status or overwrite the file yourself. Knowledge check · Is comparing an examination aggregate the same as approving admission?No. The screenshot identifies aggregate comparison in submission review. The manual separates administrative screening/selection from the admission result, and the interface has a separate Decision Management page. M §10.3 Key takeaway: Review submitted evidence first; treat final result handling as a separate controlled step."}, {"id": "lesson5", "number": 5, "title": "Decisions, reporting, and new-student intake", "subtitle": "Understand the result boundary and what the sources leave open.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">5</div><div><h2>Decisions, reporting, and new-student intake</h2><p>Understand the result boundary and what the sources leave open.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Find matched applications and decision records.</li><li>Explain the relationship between a decision, a report, publication, and student creation.</li><li>Identify corrections and duplicate identity as escalation points.</li></ul></div>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Decision and downstream handoff · conceptual, policy-dependent</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Reviewed application</strong><small>Evidence and aggregate checked</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Institutional decision</strong><small>Approved screening policy and authorised actor</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Admission result</strong><small>Result update / publication controls to verify</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>Admission Report</strong><small>Highlights, applicant statistics, programme results</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Student intake</strong><small>Approved creation and first-login checks</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">The manual lists result update, collective processing, approved new-student creation, and first-login regression checks; it does not prove a single automatic click-through chain. The report is an oversight view, not itself proof of result publication. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Page / action</th><th>Visible or documented use</th><th>Decision point and exception</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Admission Decision Management</td><td>Review matched applications; filter by year, semester, programme, examination type. A separate screenshot crop shows School, Result = “Pass”, Search, and Cancel.</td><td>The meaning of “Pass” is unconfirmed. Do not equate it with admitted, eligible, or examination pass without owner confirmation.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Result update and collective processing</td><td>Representative backend functions are named in M §10.3.</td><td>Who may approve, how a result is corrected, and whether bulk processing is atomic must be tested and governed.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admission Report</td><td>Search by academic year, semester, school, and programme. Page subtitle promises highlights, applicant statistics, and programme-level results.</td><td>Reconcile unexpected totals against filters and source records; exact measures and calculation rules are not supplied.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>New-student creation</td><td>The manual’s conceptual flow ends in approved new-student creation/enrollment intake. The completion report says “Auto Student ID.”</td><td>The trigger, timing, duplicate-identity resolution, and approval evidence are not given. Confirm before promising a student ID.</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"screenpair\"><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image8.png\" alt=\"Admission Decision Management result filter showing Pass and Search button\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 7 · Decision page crop. “Pass” is visible in a Result filter; semantics are unverified.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"screen\"><img src=\"assets/image9.png\" alt=\"Admission Report page showing year, semester, school, programme, Search and Cancel\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>UI figure 8 · Admission Report filter form and Search/Cancel buttons.</figcaption></figure></div>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · approved outcome needs follow-through</h4><p>Ama’s record is matched for 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science. With an authorised decision-maker, confirm the approved outcome and record the decision through the permitted procedure. Search the Admission Report for the same cohort and check whether its figures reflect the expected result. If Ama cannot see a published result or does not receive a student identifier, collect the reference, time, expected/actual outcome, and relevant screen evidence. Escalate to the Admissions owner and application support instead of changing tables directly. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Does a “Pass” filter value prove that an applicant has been admitted?</summary><p>No. The screenshot only proves that “Pass” appears in the Result field. The manual does not define the value or its relation to publication and student creation.</p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Decision, publication, report, and student creation are related but distinct checkpoints. Confirm their approved sequence and evidence.</div>\n    ", "search": "5Decisions, reporting, and new-student intakeUnderstand the result boundary and what the sources leave open. Learning objectivesFind matched applications and decision records.Explain the relationship between a decision, a report, publication, and student creation.Identify corrections and duplicate identity as escalation points. Decision and downstream handoff · conceptual, policy-dependentReviewed applicationEvidence and aggregate checked→Institutional decisionApproved screening policy and authorised actor→Admission resultResult update / publication controls to verify→Admission ReportHighlights, applicant statistics, programme results→Student intakeApproved creation and first-login checks The manual lists result update, collective processing, approved new-student creation, and first-login regression checks; it does not prove a single automatic click-through chain. The report is an oversight view, not itself proof of result publication. M §10.3 Page / actionVisible or documented useDecision point and exception Admission Decision ManagementReview matched applications; filter by year, semester, programme, examination type. A separate screenshot crop shows School, Result = “Pass”, Search, and Cancel.The meaning of “Pass” is unconfirmed. Do not equate it with admitted, eligible, or examination pass without owner confirmation. Result update and collective processingRepresentative backend functions are named in M §10.3.Who may approve, how a result is corrected, and whether bulk processing is atomic must be tested and governed. Admission ReportSearch by academic year, semester, school, and programme. Page subtitle promises highlights, applicant statistics, and programme-level results.Reconcile unexpected totals against filters and source records; exact measures and calculation rules are not supplied. New-student creationThe manual’s conceptual flow ends in approved new-student creation/enrollment intake. The completion report says “Auto Student ID.”The trigger, timing, duplicate-identity resolution, and approval evidence are not given. Confirm before promising a student ID. UI figure 7 · Decision page crop. “Pass” is visible in a Result filter; semantics are unverified.UI figure 8 · Admission Report filter form and Search/Cancel buttons. Worked example · approved outcome needs follow-throughAma’s record is matched for 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science. With an authorised decision-maker, confirm the approved outcome and record the decision through the permitted procedure. Search the Admission Report for the same cohort and check whether its figures reflect the expected result. If Ama cannot see a published result or does not receive a student identifier, collect the reference, time, expected/actual outcome, and relevant screen evidence. Escalate to the Admissions owner and application support instead of changing tables directly. M §10.3 Knowledge check · Does a “Pass” filter value prove that an applicant has been admitted?No. The screenshot only proves that “Pass” appears in the Result field. The manual does not define the value or its relation to publication and student creation. Key takeaway: Decision, publication, report, and student creation are related but distinct checkpoints. Confirm their approved sequence and evidence."}, {"id": "lesson6", "number": 6, "title": "Departments, suppliers, applications, and data handoffs", "subtitle": "Know which boundary to investigate before escalating.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">6</div><div><h2>Departments, suppliers, applications, and data handoffs</h2><p>Know which boundary to investigate before escalating.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Explain the main admissions data path and downstream effects.</li><li>Separate business ownership from technical support.</li><li>Use admission data-object names as a support map, not an invitation to edit records.</li></ul></div>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Handoff lanes · documented links and recommended coordination</div><div class=\"swim\"><div class=\"lane\">Lane</div><div class=\"lane\">Prepare</div><div class=\"lane\">Submit</div><div class=\"lane\">Decide</div><div class=\"lane\">Intake</div><div>Admissions owner</div><div class=\"step\">Approve criteria and period policy</div><div class=\"empty\">Resolve policy questions</div><div class=\"step\">Authorise screening and correction</div><div class=\"step\">Approve handoff rule</div><div>Applicant / public site</div><div class=\"empty\">See open intake and materials</div><div class=\"step\">Create application and attach evidence</div><div class=\"step\">Access result as supported</div><div class=\"empty\">Await student access</div><div>Staff Admin Portal</div><div class=\"step\">Schedule · criteria · support form</div><div class=\"step\">Review submissions</div><div class=\"step\">Decision Management · report</div><div class=\"step\">Verify approved transfer</div><div>Backend / Oracle</div><div class=\"step\">Rules and programme data</div><div class=\"step\">Application and file records</div><div class=\"step\">Result updates / collective processing</div><div class=\"step\">Student master and login dependency</div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">The lane order is an interpretation of M §10.3 and the six UI screens. Formal role assignment, approval route, and the exact transfer action remain open questions. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Data or system</th><th>Why it matters to Admission</th><th>Operational action</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>School / Programme master</td><td>Filters and open-programme check depend on the School–Programme relationship. M §10.2 names IT_DEPT_UNIV and IT_DEPT_INFO.</td><td>If a programme is missing, capture cohort and screenshot; verify the approved master record with the owning office. Do not create a duplicate programme.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Admission records and files</td><td>M §10.2 lists IT_ADM_APP, IT_ADM_APPUSER, IT_ADM_APPFILE, IT_ADM_REQUIREMENTS, and IT_ADM_INSTITUTIONS.</td><td>Use these names to route a technical investigation. The manual does not define every column or status.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Student master / student portal</td><td>Accepted applicant creation feeds student intake; first login is a required regression check. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td><td>When a decision is correct but student access fails, check the handoff and identity duplication with authorised staff.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Identity, menus, SSO</td><td>Admin role/menu and server session affect staff access; the completion report describes single sign-on (SSO) across systems. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-16-authentication-and-authorization\">M §1.6</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=13\">P p. 13</a></td><td>Separate “cannot sign in” from “can sign in but cannot see Admission.” Confirm current integration design with identity support.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Supplier and local IT</td><td>Completion report identifies local UEAS IT as first-line support, Korean remote support, and specialist vendors. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=16\">P p. 16</a></td><td>Send a reproducible, privacy-safe packet to the named support contact in the protected operations record. Do not guess the current contract or contact.</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"gap\"><span class=\"tag unknown\">OPEN QUESTION</span>Source identity and vendor details conflict. The PDF filename begins “UESD,” while its cover says “Ghana UEAS” and its project description refers to an environment and sustainability university. The operator role document names UESD and assigns GAS to Simsys Global, while the completion report assigns GAS to DB Inc. Confirm the official institution, current supplier matrix, and contract contacts before relying on these labels operationally. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=1\">P p. 1</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=5\">P p. 5</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/IT%20Application%20System%20Operator.docx\">R</a></div>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · decision exists, student record does not</h4><p>For fictional <strong>Kofi Owusu</strong>, Admissions confirms a positive decision, but no student identifier appears. Record the application reference, programme, cohort, approved decision evidence, and time. Ask the designated owner whether creation is a separate approved step. Application support should check duplicate identity handling and the admission-to-student transfer. Verify the resulting student record and first login only after the authorised fix. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · Why should a missing student ID not be “fixed” by creating a second applicant?</summary><p>The manual explicitly calls for duplicate identity handling and approved new-student creation. A second applicant could conceal the broken handoff and create duplicate records. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Trace the boundary where the expected record should originate; obtain the business owner’s decision before a persistent correction.</div>\n    ", "search": "6Departments, suppliers, applications, and data handoffsKnow which boundary to investigate before escalating. Learning objectivesExplain the main admissions data path and downstream effects.Separate business ownership from technical support.Use admission data-object names as a support map, not an invitation to edit records. Handoff lanes · documented links and recommended coordinationLanePrepareSubmitDecideIntakeAdmissions ownerApprove criteria and period policyResolve policy questionsAuthorise screening and correctionApprove handoff ruleApplicant / public siteSee open intake and materialsCreate application and attach evidenceAccess result as supportedAwait student accessStaff Admin PortalSchedule · criteria · support formReview submissionsDecision Management · reportVerify approved transferBackend / OracleRules and programme dataApplication and file recordsResult updates / collective processingStudent master and login dependency The lane order is an interpretation of M §10.3 and the six UI screens. Formal role assignment, approval route, and the exact transfer action remain open questions. M §10.3 Data or systemWhy it matters to AdmissionOperational action School / Programme masterFilters and open-programme check depend on the School–Programme relationship. M §10.2 names IT_DEPT_UNIV and IT_DEPT_INFO.If a programme is missing, capture cohort and screenshot; verify the approved master record with the owning office. Do not create a duplicate programme. Admission records and filesM §10.2 lists IT_ADM_APP, IT_ADM_APPUSER, IT_ADM_APPFILE, IT_ADM_REQUIREMENTS, and IT_ADM_INSTITUTIONS.Use these names to route a technical investigation. The manual does not define every column or status. Student master / student portalAccepted applicant creation feeds student intake; first login is a required regression check. M §10.3When a decision is correct but student access fails, check the handoff and identity duplication with authorised staff. Identity, menus, SSOAdmin role/menu and server session affect staff access; the completion report describes single sign-on (SSO) across systems. M §1.6 P p. 13Separate “cannot sign in” from “can sign in but cannot see Admission.” Confirm current integration design with identity support. Supplier and local ITCompletion report identifies local UEAS IT as first-line support, Korean remote support, and specialist vendors. P p. 16Send a reproducible, privacy-safe packet to the named support contact in the protected operations record. Do not guess the current contract or contact. OPEN QUESTIONSource identity and vendor details conflict. The PDF filename begins “UESD,” while its cover says “Ghana UEAS” and its project description refers to an environment and sustainability university. The operator role document names UESD and assigns GAS to Simsys Global, while the completion report assigns GAS to DB Inc. Confirm the official institution, current supplier matrix, and contract contacts before relying on these labels operationally. P p. 1 P p. 5 R Worked example · decision exists, student record does notFor fictional Kofi Owusu, Admissions confirms a positive decision, but no student identifier appears. Record the application reference, programme, cohort, approved decision evidence, and time. Ask the designated owner whether creation is a separate approved step. Application support should check duplicate identity handling and the admission-to-student transfer. Verify the resulting student record and first login only after the authorised fix. M §10.3 Knowledge check · Why should a missing student ID not be “fixed” by creating a second applicant?The manual explicitly calls for duplicate identity handling and approved new-student creation. A second applicant could conceal the broken handoff and create duplicate records. M §10.3 Key takeaway: Trace the boundary where the expected record should originate; obtain the business owner’s decision before a persistent correction."}, {"id": "lesson7", "number": 7, "title": "Routine operation, user support, and escalation", "subtitle": "Turn a user complaint into a safe, testable service record.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">7</div><div><h2>Routine operation, user support, and escalation</h2><p>Turn a user complaint into a safe, testable service record.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Perform a practical read-only intake check.</li><li>Separate data, policy, access, file, and application faults.</li><li>Escalate with enough evidence and protect applicant information.</li></ul></div>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>The manual’s production investigation order is: capture exact time, role, URL, filters, expected/actual result; reproduce read-only; inspect the request; trace the owning layer; verify data with authorised read-only access; then fix the owning layer and add a regression check. It also requires file-storage policy, monitoring, and incident runbooks in a protected operations record. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-06-65-investigating-a-production-issue\">M §6.5</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-129-file-storage-and-retention\">M §12.9</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-1211-incident-and-recovery-runbooks\">M §12.11</a></p>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">First-line service flow · recommended</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Receive request</strong><small>Who, when, cohort, URL, expected vs actual</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Verify safely</strong><small>Role, filters, read-only reproduction, source record</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Classify</strong><small>Policy / data / access / file / application</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>Route to owner</strong><small>Business approval or technical support</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Retest and close</strong><small>Confirm with user; record evidence</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">This is a proposed service pattern based on M §6.5 and the operator role description, not a prescribed university ticket workflow. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/IT%20Application%20System%20Operator.docx\">R</a></p>\n      <h3>Recommended routine checks</h3>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Check</th><th>Evidence and action</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Before an intake opens</td><td>Schedule, criteria, and support materials agree for the same cohort.</td><td>Record dates and page evidence; send mismatches to Admissions before public use. <span class=\"tag reco\">RECOMMENDATION</span></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>During the intake</td><td>Authorised staff access, application search, attachment availability, and reported submission issues.</td><td>Use a safe test record and log incident time, filters, URL, role, and status. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-05-58-logs-and-evidence\">M §5.8</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>During decision processing</td><td>Decision corrections, batch actions, duplicate identity, result publication, and student-creation exceptions.</td><td>Require policy approval and traceability; do not assume partial batch success is acceptable. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>After a change or release</td><td>Staff login/menu, a read-only AAS query, uploads, result path, and approved test scenario.</td><td>The manual has a general post-deployment smoke test; add admission-specific checks to the local plan. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-05-56-post-deployment-smoke-test\">M §5.6</a></td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <h3>Troubleshooting quick map</h3>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>First safe check</th><th>Likely owner / stop point</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>Staff cannot see Admission</td><td>Confirm staff role, session, authorised menu response, and whether other AAS menus appear.</td><td>Identity/menu administrator. Hidden menu is not proof of backend permission. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-16-authentication-and-authorization\">M §1.6</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Page opens but API fails or returns HTML</td><td>Capture exact URL, time, browser response code; compare browser route with API route.</td><td>Application/web-server support. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-06-64-troubleshooting-matrix\">M §6.4</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Programme or application missing</td><td>Confirm year, semester, school, programme, examination type; check approved programme status.</td><td>Admissions/master-data owner, then application support if source is correct. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Attachment cannot be viewed</td><td>Record affected application reference and file action; do not collect the full identity document in a normal ticket.</td><td>File/storage and application support; use protected access. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-129-file-storage-and-retention\">M §12.9</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Wrong result or report total</td><td>Confirm cohort filters and approved decision evidence; reproduce read-only.</td><td>Admissions owner for business correction; application support for mismatch. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Accepted applicant cannot log in</td><td>Confirm decision, student-creation step, account state, and exact login error without requesting password.</td><td>Admissions/student-record owner and identity support. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"box\"><h3>Escalation packet</h3><p><span class=\"tag reco\">RECOMMENDATION</span>Include incident reference; time and time zone; affected role and anonymised applicant reference; page URL; year/semester/school/programme/examination filters; expected and actual result; reproduction steps; HTTP status or error text; impact and urgency; business owner’s decision or approval; screenshots redacted of identity documents; tests already run; and the requested next action. The manual requires the protected operations record to name actual contacts and escalation chains. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-05-58-logs-and-evidence\">M §5.8</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-122-access-request-and-escalation\">M §12.2</a></p></div>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><h4>Worked example · application missing from staff list</h4><p>Ama reports a successful submission but staff cannot find TEST-ADM-027. First record the confirmation evidence and time. Check the staff filter year, semester, school, programme, and exam type. If still missing, reproduce read-only and capture the list request and response status. Ask the Admissions owner whether the applicant submitted to the intended intake; send a redacted support packet if source and display disagree. Do not ask Ama to submit a second application until duplicate-handling rules are confirmed.</p></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · What belongs in an ordinary support ticket?</summary><p>The minimum reproducible facts, role, filters, time, URL, error, and an anonymised reference. Passwords, session IDs, full identity documents, and unnecessary personal data do not. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-05-59-security-checklist\">M §5.9</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Verify before changing; obtain the business decision from its owner; escalate a reproducible, privacy-safe fault; retest the actual user journey.</div>\n    ", "search": "7Routine operation, user support, and escalationTurn a user complaint into a safe, testable service record. Learning objectivesPerform a practical read-only intake check.Separate data, policy, access, file, and application faults.Escalate with enough evidence and protect applicant information. DOCUMENTEDThe manual’s production investigation order is: capture exact time, role, URL, filters, expected/actual result; reproduce read-only; inspect the request; trace the owning layer; verify data with authorised read-only access; then fix the owning layer and add a regression check. It also requires file-storage policy, monitoring, and incident runbooks in a protected operations record. M §6.5 M §12.9 M §12.11 First-line service flow · recommendedReceive requestWho, when, cohort, URL, expected vs actual→Verify safelyRole, filters, read-only reproduction, source record→ClassifyPolicy / data / access / file / application→Route to ownerBusiness approval or technical support→Retest and closeConfirm with user; record evidence This is a proposed service pattern based on M §6.5 and the operator role description, not a prescribed university ticket workflow. R Recommended routine checks WhenCheckEvidence and action Before an intake opensSchedule, criteria, and support materials agree for the same cohort.Record dates and page evidence; send mismatches to Admissions before public use. RECOMMENDATION During the intakeAuthorised staff access, application search, attachment availability, and reported submission issues.Use a safe test record and log incident time, filters, URL, role, and status. M §5.8 During decision processingDecision corrections, batch actions, duplicate identity, result publication, and student-creation exceptions.Require policy approval and traceability; do not assume partial batch success is acceptable. M §10.3 After a change or releaseStaff login/menu, a read-only AAS query, uploads, result path, and approved test scenario.The manual has a general post-deployment smoke test; add admission-specific checks to the local plan. M §5.6 Troubleshooting quick map SymptomFirst safe checkLikely owner / stop point Staff cannot see AdmissionConfirm staff role, session, authorised menu response, and whether other AAS menus appear.Identity/menu administrator. Hidden menu is not proof of backend permission. M §1.6 Page opens but API fails or returns HTMLCapture exact URL, time, browser response code; compare browser route with API route.Application/web-server support. M §6.4 Programme or application missingConfirm year, semester, school, programme, examination type; check approved programme status.Admissions/master-data owner, then application support if source is correct. M §10.3 Attachment cannot be viewedRecord affected application reference and file action; do not collect the full identity document in a normal ticket.File/storage and application support; use protected access. M §12.9 Wrong result or report totalConfirm cohort filters and approved decision evidence; reproduce read-only.Admissions owner for business correction; application support for mismatch. M §10.11 Accepted applicant cannot log inConfirm decision, student-creation step, account state, and exact login error without requesting password.Admissions/student-record owner and identity support. M §10.3 Escalation packetRECOMMENDATIONInclude incident reference; time and time zone; affected role and anonymised applicant reference; page URL; year/semester/school/programme/examination filters; expected and actual result; reproduction steps; HTTP status or error text; impact and urgency; business owner’s decision or approval; screenshots redacted of identity documents; tests already run; and the requested next action. The manual requires the protected operations record to name actual contacts and escalation chains. M §5.8 M §12.2 Worked example · application missing from staff listAma reports a successful submission but staff cannot find TEST-ADM-027. First record the confirmation evidence and time. Check the staff filter year, semester, school, programme, and exam type. If still missing, reproduce read-only and capture the list request and response status. Ask the Admissions owner whether the applicant submitted to the intended intake; send a redacted support packet if source and display disagree. Do not ask Ama to submit a second application until duplicate-handling rules are confirmed. Knowledge check · What belongs in an ordinary support ticket?The minimum reproducible facts, role, filters, time, URL, error, and an anonymised reference. Passwords, session IDs, full identity documents, and unnecessary personal data do not. M §5.9 Key takeaway: Verify before changing; obtain the business decision from its owner; escalate a reproducible, privacy-safe fault; retest the actual user journey."}, {"id": "lesson8", "number": 8, "title": "Practice lab and meeting preparation", "subtitle": "Use fictional cases to explain the portal with confidence.", "html": "<div class=\"lesson-head\"><div class=\"lesson-num\">8</div><div><h2>Practice lab and meeting preparation</h2><p>Use fictional cases to explain the portal with confidence.</p></div></div>\n      <div class=\"box objectives\"><h3>Learning objectives</h3><ul><li>Work through intake, review, decision, and incident cases.</li><li>Answer common team-lead questions without overclaiming.</li><li>Bring unresolved policy and technical questions to the right owner.</li></ul></div>\n      <div class=\"diagram\"><div class=\"diagram-title\">Recall path for every meeting case</div><div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>Identify cohort</strong><small>Year · semester · school · programme</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node green\"><strong>Identify stage</strong><small>Prepare · submit · review · decide · report · intake</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node gold\"><strong>Find owner</strong><small>Business approval or technical support</small></div><div class=\"arrow\">→</div><div class=\"node\"><strong>State evidence</strong><small>Documented · screenshot · interpretation · gap</small></div></div></div>\n      <p class=\"diagram-caption\">This is a study aid synthesised from the preceding lessons, not a system process diagram.</p>\n      <h3>Scenario 1 · Intake readiness</h3>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><p><strong>Fictional situation:</strong> The 2027 Semester 2 Agricultural Technology period is visible, but staff cannot confirm whether the current application form file belongs to that cohort.</p><p><strong>Questions:</strong> Which pages do you compare? Who authorises a correction? What outcome closes the check?</p><p><strong>Worked answer:</strong> Compare Schedule Management, Criteria Settings, and Register Support Form using the same year, semester, school, and programme. Obtain the approved criteria and file version from Admissions. If the association is wrong, route the correction to the authorised content/file operator with owner approval. Close only after the correct form can be found for the intended cohort and the check is recorded. <span class=\"tag reco\">RECOMMENDATION</span></p></div>\n      <h3>Scenario 2 · Submission review</h3>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><p><strong>Fictional situation:</strong> Kofi Owusu (TEST-ADM-042) applied for Civil Engineering using WASSCE (International). Staff searched only WASSCE (Ghanaian) and found no record.</p><p><strong>Questions:</strong> What is the first correction? What should not be concluded?</p><p><strong>Worked answer:</strong> Recheck the 2027 cohort and select WASSCE (International), along with the correct school and programme. A missing result under the wrong exam filter does not prove that Kofi failed to submit. If the record remains absent, collect submission evidence and escalate. <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span> UI figure 6.</p></div>\n      <h3>Scenario 3 · Result and report mismatch</h3>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><p><strong>Fictional situation:</strong> An authorised Admissions officer says Ama’s decision was corrected, but a 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science report still shows the earlier count.</p><p><strong>Questions:</strong> What do you verify? Can you simply edit the report total?</p><p><strong>Worked answer:</strong> Confirm the approved correction record, cohort filters, whether Search has been applied, and whether the underlying applicant/result record matches. Note time and any caching or API error evidence. Escalate the mismatch to the application owner; do not edit a report total as a substitute for correcting its source. The manual requires an approved policy for result correction. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></p></div>\n      <h3>Scenario 4 · First login fails</h3>\n      <div class=\"exercise\"><p><strong>Fictional situation:</strong> A positive admission result is visible to applicant Yaa Boateng, but first student login fails.</p><p><strong>Questions:</strong> Which boundary might have failed? What evidence do you send?</p><p><strong>Worked answer:</strong> Confirm the approved result, check whether the new-student creation step completed, then check identity/account state through authorised staff. Send the application reference, cohort, decision evidence, exact error and time—never a password. The manual specifically calls for first-login and duplicate-identity regression checks. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></div>\n      <h3>Questions a team lead may ask</h3>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Likely question</th><th>Suggested answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>What are the two staff work areas?</td><td>Application Management prepares dates, criteria, and support materials. Acceptance Management reviews submissions, manages decisions, and reports outcomes. The six menu names come from the supplied screenshots.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Where does an applicant apply?</td><td>Through the public website admission feature; staff use the Admin Portal. Both use the existing backend. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">M §1.1</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Which filters identify one intake?</td><td>Academic year and semester, usually narrowed by school and programme; submission/decision review may also need examination type. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-03-31-terminology\">M §3.1</a> and UI figures.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Does “Pass” mean admitted?</td><td>Not established. It is visible as a Result filter value on the decision page; its code and policy meaning need confirmation.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Who can change a result?</td><td>The manual requires an approved admissions-screening and result-correction policy with a named institutional owner. It does not assign my role independent approval authority. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>When is a student ID created?</td><td>The completion report advertises automatic student ID creation, while the manual says approved new-student creation follows the admission result. The exact trigger and safeguards must be demonstrated locally. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=6\">P p. 6</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>What does first-line support do?</td><td>Capture the precise issue, reproduce read-only where possible, identify whether it is policy, data, access, file, or technical, route it to the owner, and verify the outcome. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-06-65-investigating-a-production-issue\">M §6.5</a></td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <details><summary>Knowledge check · State the admission chain in one sentence.</summary><p>An applicant supplies an application and evidence; staff review and decide under approved criteria; results are managed and reported; an approved applicant is transferred into new-student intake, with the exact publication and creation controls still to be confirmed. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a></p></details>\n      <div class=\"key\"><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> A strong meeting answer names the stage, the source of truth, the responsible owner, the evidence, and the unresolved rule.</div>\n    ", "search": "8Practice lab and meeting preparationUse fictional cases to explain the portal with confidence. Learning objectivesWork through intake, review, decision, and incident cases.Answer common team-lead questions without overclaiming.Bring unresolved policy and technical questions to the right owner. Recall path for every meeting caseIdentify cohortYear · semester · school · programme→Identify stagePrepare · submit · review · decide · report · intake→Find ownerBusiness approval or technical support→State evidenceDocumented · screenshot · interpretation · gap This is a study aid synthesised from the preceding lessons, not a system process diagram. Scenario 1 · Intake readiness Fictional situation: The 2027 Semester 2 Agricultural Technology period is visible, but staff cannot confirm whether the current application form file belongs to that cohort.Questions: Which pages do you compare? Who authorises a correction? What outcome closes the check?Worked answer: Compare Schedule Management, Criteria Settings, and Register Support Form using the same year, semester, school, and programme. Obtain the approved criteria and file version from Admissions. If the association is wrong, route the correction to the authorised content/file operator with owner approval. Close only after the correct form can be found for the intended cohort and the check is recorded. RECOMMENDATION Scenario 2 · Submission review Fictional situation: Kofi Owusu (TEST-ADM-042) applied for Civil Engineering using WASSCE (International). Staff searched only WASSCE (Ghanaian) and found no record.Questions: What is the first correction? What should not be concluded?Worked answer: Recheck the 2027 cohort and select WASSCE (International), along with the correct school and programme. A missing result under the wrong exam filter does not prove that Kofi failed to submit. If the record remains absent, collect submission evidence and escalate. SCREENSHOT UI figure 6. Scenario 3 · Result and report mismatch Fictional situation: An authorised Admissions officer says Ama’s decision was corrected, but a 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science report still shows the earlier count.Questions: What do you verify? Can you simply edit the report total?Worked answer: Confirm the approved correction record, cohort filters, whether Search has been applied, and whether the underlying applicant/result record matches. Note time and any caching or API error evidence. Escalate the mismatch to the application owner; do not edit a report total as a substitute for correcting its source. The manual requires an approved policy for result correction. M §10.11 Scenario 4 · First login fails Fictional situation: A positive admission result is visible to applicant Yaa Boateng, but first student login fails.Questions: Which boundary might have failed? What evidence do you send?Worked answer: Confirm the approved result, check whether the new-student creation step completed, then check identity/account state through authorised staff. Send the application reference, cohort, decision evidence, exact error and time—never a password. The manual specifically calls for first-login and duplicate-identity regression checks. M §10.3 Questions a team lead may ask Likely questionSuggested answer What are the two staff work areas?Application Management prepares dates, criteria, and support materials. Acceptance Management reviews submissions, manages decisions, and reports outcomes. The six menu names come from the supplied screenshots. Where does an applicant apply?Through the public website admission feature; staff use the Admin Portal. Both use the existing backend. M §1.1 Which filters identify one intake?Academic year and semester, usually narrowed by school and programme; submission/decision review may also need examination type. M §3.1 and UI figures. Does “Pass” mean admitted?Not established. It is visible as a Result filter value on the decision page; its code and policy meaning need confirmation. Who can change a result?The manual requires an approved admissions-screening and result-correction policy with a named institutional owner. It does not assign my role independent approval authority. M §10.11 When is a student ID created?The completion report advertises automatic student ID creation, while the manual says approved new-student creation follows the admission result. The exact trigger and safeguards must be demonstrated locally. P p. 6 M §10.3 What does first-line support do?Capture the precise issue, reproduce read-only where possible, identify whether it is policy, data, access, file, or technical, route it to the owner, and verify the outcome. M §6.5 Knowledge check · State the admission chain in one sentence.An applicant supplies an application and evidence; staff review and decide under approved criteria; results are managed and reported; an approved applicant is transferred into new-student intake, with the exact publication and creation controls still to be confirmed. M §10.3 Key takeaway: A strong meeting answer names the stage, the source of truth, the responsible owner, the evidence, and the unresolved rule."}], "panels": {"map": {"html": "<h2>Admission topic map and recommended study order</h2>\n      <p><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span>The technical manual identifies a public admission application on the homepage, staff admission functions in the Academic Administration System (AAS) Admin Portal, and a conceptual path from applicant account to approved new-student intake. Its main admissions map is <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a>; architecture is in <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">M §1.1</a>. The six staff screens below are evidenced by the accompanying Admission Portal document and screenshots.</p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Lesson</th><th>Core question</th><th>Main source</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>1</td><td>Portal landscape</td><td>Where does Admission sit, and who uses it?</td><td>M §§1.1, 1.9, 10.2; P pp. 5–6</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>2</td><td>Access and screens</td><td>How do I find the right page and interpret filters?</td><td>M §§1.6, 3.1; UI screenshots</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>3</td><td>Prepare an intake</td><td>How do timing, criteria, and materials align?</td><td>M §10.3; UI screens 1–3</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>4</td><td>Receive and review</td><td>What happens from applicant submission to screening?</td><td>M §§10.3, 6.2; UI screen 4</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>5</td><td>Decide and report</td><td>How do results, reporting, and student intake relate?</td><td>M §§10.3–10.4; UI screens 5–6; P p. 6</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>6</td><td>Handoffs and data</td><td>Which people, systems, and records depend on Admission?</td><td>M §§10.2–10.3, 12.1; P pp. 5, 13, 16</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>7</td><td>Operate and support</td><td>How do I detect, investigate, and escalate issues?</td><td>M §§5.8–5.10, 6.4–6.5, 12.9–12.11</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>8</td><td>Practise and explain</td><td>Can I work through cases and answer meeting questions?</td><td>Synthesis of the cited sources</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <p class=\"mini\">Study plan: one lesson per sitting. After Lessons 3 and 5, repeat the worked example in a permitted test environment. Do not perform write exercises against live applicant data without the authorised account and approved test plan. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-11-118-role-and-account-acquisition\">M §11.8</a></p>\n    ", "search": "Admission topic map and recommended study order DOCUMENTEDThe technical manual identifies a public admission application on the homepage, staff admission functions in the Academic Administration System (AAS) Admin Portal, and a conceptual path from applicant account to approved new-student intake. Its main admissions map is M §10.3; architecture is in M §1.1. The six staff screens below are evidenced by the accompanying Admission Portal document and screenshots. OrderLessonCore questionMain source 1Portal landscapeWhere does Admission sit, and who uses it?M §§1.1, 1.9, 10.2; P pp. 5–6 2Access and screensHow do I find the right page and interpret filters?M §§1.6, 3.1; UI screenshots 3Prepare an intakeHow do timing, criteria, and materials align?M §10.3; UI screens 1–3 4Receive and reviewWhat happens from applicant submission to screening?M §§10.3, 6.2; UI screen 4 5Decide and reportHow do results, reporting, and student intake relate?M §§10.3–10.4; UI screens 5–6; P p. 6 6Handoffs and dataWhich people, systems, and records depend on Admission?M §§10.2–10.3, 12.1; P pp. 5, 13, 16 7Operate and supportHow do I detect, investigate, and escalate issues?M §§5.8–5.10, 6.4–6.5, 12.9–12.11 8Practise and explainCan I work through cases and answer meeting questions?Synthesis of the cited sources Study plan: one lesson per sitting. After Lessons 3 and 5, repeat the worked example in a permitted test environment. Do not perform write exercises against live applicant data without the authorised account and approved test plan. M §11.8"}, "evidence": {"html": "<h2>How to read the evidence</h2>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>How to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td><span class=\"tag doc\">DOCUMENTED</span></td><td>Stated by the technical manual or completion report.</td><td>Read the linked section; the technical manual itself says policy still needs UEAS approval.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span></td><td>Visible in the supplied staff-screen images.</td><td>A visible filter or caption is evidence; hidden controls and exact save behaviour are not.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><span class=\"tag interp\">INTERPRETATION</span></td><td>A reasoned connection between source facts.</td><td>Use it as a learning model, then verify locally.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><span class=\"tag reco\">RECOMMENDATION</span></td><td>A proposed operating step for this role.</td><td>Confirm it with the Admissions owner and local service process.</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><span class=\"tag unknown\">OPEN QUESTION</span></td><td>Missing, ambiguous, or conflicting information.</td><td>Do not turn it into an assumed portal rule.</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <p><strong>Source shorthand:</strong> M = <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-readme-ueas-developer-handover\">Ghana UEAS Technical Manual</a>; P = <a href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf\">CIS Completion Report</a>; UI = screenshots embedded in <a href=\"sources/UEAS%20Admission%20Portal.docx\">UEAS Admission Portal.docx</a>; R = <a href=\"sources/IT%20Application%20System%20Operator.docx\">operator role description</a>. Source links and caveats are listed at the end.</p>\n    ", "search": "How to read the evidence LabelMeaningHow to use it DOCUMENTEDStated by the technical manual or completion report.Read the linked section; the technical manual itself says policy still needs UEAS approval. SCREENSHOTVisible in the supplied staff-screen images.A visible filter or caption is evidence; hidden controls and exact save behaviour are not. INTERPRETATIONA reasoned connection between source facts.Use it as a learning model, then verify locally. RECOMMENDATIONA proposed operating step for this role.Confirm it with the Admissions owner and local service process. OPEN QUESTIONMissing, ambiguous, or conflicting information.Do not turn it into an assumed portal rule. Source shorthand: M = Ghana UEAS Technical Manual; P = CIS Completion Report; UI = screenshots embedded in UEAS Admission Portal.docx; R = operator role description. Source links and caveats are listed at the end."}, "gaps": {"html": "<h2>Questions to resolve before independent operation</h2>\n      <p><span class=\"tag unknown\">OPEN QUESTION</span>These are deliberately unanswered because the supplied sources do not establish a reliable rule or current live-state proof.</p>\n      <div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question to raise</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Proposed owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n        <tr><td>What are the approved criteria, aggregate formula, attachment list, and correction process for each intake?</td><td>Prevents inconsistent screening and unapproved result changes.</td><td>Admissions / Registrar policy owner</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>What does each application, review, “Pass,” decision, and publication status mean?</td><td>The sources show only one visible Result value and no complete status dictionary.</td><td>Admissions owner + application support</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Who approves schedule, criteria, support-form, decision, and batch changes, and where is approval recorded?</td><td>The manual explicitly requires an institutional approver and auditability.</td><td>Admissions / Registrar + identity administrator</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>How is an applicant notified, how is the result published, and what triggers student ID creation?</td><td>Required to explain the end-to-end applicant journey and diagnose a missing account.</td><td>Admissions + student records + application support</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>What happens on missing attachments, duplicates, partial batch failure, appeals, and result correction?</td><td>These are named risks or policy gaps; no click-by-click exception path is supplied.</td><td>Admissions policy owner + technical owner</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>What are the live portal URL, current roles, support contacts, file retention rules, Ghana time-zone policy, and response targets?</td><td>The protected operations inventory calls for these but does not contain their values.</td><td>Local IT / service owner <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-121-document-control\">M §12.1</a></td></tr>\n        <tr><td>Which institution and vendor names are authoritative for this deployment?</td><td>The PDF filename/description and role document conflict with the UEAS manual and with each other.</td><td>Project owner / procurement / local IT</td></tr>\n      </tbody></table></div>\n      <div class=\"gap danger\"><strong>Operational stop condition:</strong> Do not change persistent admissions behaviour or announce a result-correction rule based only on this study manual. The technical manual says it is a handover, not an institutional policy, and calls for written approval where policy is absent or contradictory. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a></div>\n    ", "search": "Questions to resolve before independent operation OPEN QUESTIONThese are deliberately unanswered because the supplied sources do not establish a reliable rule or current live-state proof. Question to raiseWhy it mattersProposed owner What are the approved criteria, aggregate formula, attachment list, and correction process for each intake?Prevents inconsistent screening and unapproved result changes.Admissions / Registrar policy owner What does each application, review, “Pass,” decision, and publication status mean?The sources show only one visible Result value and no complete status dictionary.Admissions owner + application support Who approves schedule, criteria, support-form, decision, and batch changes, and where is approval recorded?The manual explicitly requires an institutional approver and auditability.Admissions / Registrar + identity administrator How is an applicant notified, how is the result published, and what triggers student ID creation?Required to explain the end-to-end applicant journey and diagnose a missing account.Admissions + student records + application support What happens on missing attachments, duplicates, partial batch failure, appeals, and result correction?These are named risks or policy gaps; no click-by-click exception path is supplied.Admissions policy owner + technical owner What are the live portal URL, current roles, support contacts, file retention rules, Ghana time-zone policy, and response targets?The protected operations inventory calls for these but does not contain their values.Local IT / service owner M §12.1 Which institution and vendor names are authoritative for this deployment?The PDF filename/description and role document conflict with the UEAS manual and with each other.Project owner / procurement / local IT Operational stop condition: Do not change persistent admissions behaviour or announce a result-correction rule based only on this study manual. The technical manual says it is a handover, not an institutional policy, and calls for written approval where policy is absent or contradictory. M §10.11"}, "sources": {"html": "<h2>Source index and limits</h2>\n      <ol class=\"source-list\">\n        <li><strong>M · Ghana UEAS Technical Manual.</strong> Use <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">§1.1</a> for product surfaces, <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-16-authentication-and-authorization\">§1.6</a> for access, <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-03-31-terminology\">§3.1</a> for terminology, <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-102-end-to-end-ownership-matrix\">§10.2</a> for domain ownership and records, <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">§10.3</a> for the admission concept, <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">§10.11</a> for policy gaps, and <a href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-12-122-access-request-and-escalation\">§12.2</a> for escalation design. This is a developer handover, not an approved admissions user guide.</li>\n        <li><strong>P · CIS Completion Report.</strong> <a href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=5\">p. 5</a> system composition; <a href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=6\">p. 6</a> AAS Admission capabilities; <a href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=13\">p. 13</a> SSO; <a href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=16\">p. 16</a> handover support. It is a project-level claim, not proof of the current live workflow.</li>\n        <li><strong>UI · <a href=\"sources/UEAS%20Admission%20Portal.docx\">UEAS Admission Portal.docx</a>.</strong> Ten embedded images include screen crops for six staff pages. Eight relevant crops appear above. They prove visible labels and examples, not hidden save/result behaviour.</li>\n        <li><strong>R · <a href=\"sources/IT%20Application%20System%20Operator.docx\">IT Application System Operator.docx</a>.</strong> Supports the first-line operations and coordination role; its institution and supplier names require confirmation.</li>\n        <li><strong>Supporting note · <a href=\"sources/Admission%20Portal.odt\">Admission Portal.odt</a>.</strong> Places the staff Admission area inside AAS/CIS. <strong>Practical service workflow</strong> project note proposes a five-question incident method; this manual treats that as recommendation, not an approved university procedure.</li>\n      </ol>\n      <p class=\"foot\">All applicants, references, and scenarios in this guide are fictional. Companion source files are listed in the repository; the public learning website links to private copies that require GitHub access. No Google Stitch connection was available; diagrams are embedded locally and labelled where they interpret the sources.</p>\n    ", "search": "Source index and limits M · Ghana UEAS Technical Manual. Use §1.1 for product surfaces, §1.6 for access, §3.1 for terminology, §10.2 for domain ownership and records, §10.3 for the admission concept, §10.11 for policy gaps, and §12.2 for escalation design. This is a developer handover, not an approved admissions user guide. P · CIS Completion Report. p. 5 system composition; p. 6 AAS Admission capabilities; p. 13 SSO; p. 16 handover support. It is a project-level claim, not proof of the current live workflow. UI · UEAS Admission Portal.docx. Ten embedded images include screen crops for six staff pages. Eight relevant crops appear above. They prove visible labels and examples, not hidden save/result behaviour. R · IT Application System Operator.docx. Supports the first-line operations and coordination role; its institution and supplier names require confirmation. Supporting note · Admission Portal.odt. Places the staff Admission area inside AAS/CIS. Practical service workflow project note proposes a five-question incident method; this manual treats that as recommendation, not an approved university procedure. All applicants, references, and scenarios in this guide are fictional. Companion source files are listed in the repository; the public learning website links to private copies that require GitHub access. No Google Stitch connection was available; diagrams are embedded locally and labelled where they interpret the sources."}}, "scenarios": [{"id": "scenario1", "title": "Scenario 1 · Intake readiness", "situation": "<strong>Fictional situation:</strong> The 2027 Semester 2 Agricultural Technology period is visible, but staff cannot confirm whether the current application form file belongs to that cohort.", "questions": "<strong>Questions:</strong> Which pages do you compare? Who authorises a correction? What outcome closes the check?", "answer": "<strong>Worked answer:</strong> Compare Schedule Management, Criteria Settings, and Register Support Form using the same year, semester, school, and programme. Obtain the approved criteria and file version from Admissions. If the association is wrong, route the correction to the authorised content/file operator with owner approval. Close only after the correct form can be found for the intended cohort and the check is recorded. <span class=\"tag reco\">RECOMMENDATION</span>", "search": "Scenario 1 · Intake readiness Fictional situation: The 2027 Semester 2 Agricultural Technology period is visible, but staff cannot confirm whether the current application form file belongs to that cohort.Questions: Which pages do you compare? Who authorises a correction? What outcome closes the check?Worked answer: Compare Schedule Management, Criteria Settings, and Register Support Form using the same year, semester, school, and programme. Obtain the approved criteria and file version from Admissions. If the association is wrong, route the correction to the authorised content/file operator with owner approval. Close only after the correct form can be found for the intended cohort and the check is recorded. RECOMMENDATION"}, {"id": "scenario2", "title": "Scenario 2 · Submission review", "situation": "<strong>Fictional situation:</strong> Kofi Owusu (TEST-ADM-042) applied for Civil Engineering using WASSCE (International). Staff searched only WASSCE (Ghanaian) and found no record.", "questions": "<strong>Questions:</strong> What is the first correction? What should not be concluded?", "answer": "<strong>Worked answer:</strong> Recheck the 2027 cohort and select WASSCE (International), along with the correct school and programme. A missing result under the wrong exam filter does not prove that Kofi failed to submit. If the record remains absent, collect submission evidence and escalate. <span class=\"tag shot\">SCREENSHOT</span> UI figure 6.", "search": "Scenario 2 · Submission review Fictional situation: Kofi Owusu (TEST-ADM-042) applied for Civil Engineering using WASSCE (International). Staff searched only WASSCE (Ghanaian) and found no record.Questions: What is the first correction? What should not be concluded?Worked answer: Recheck the 2027 cohort and select WASSCE (International), along with the correct school and programme. A missing result under the wrong exam filter does not prove that Kofi failed to submit. If the record remains absent, collect submission evidence and escalate. SCREENSHOT UI figure 6."}, {"id": "scenario3", "title": "Scenario 3 · Result and report mismatch", "situation": "<strong>Fictional situation:</strong> An authorised Admissions officer says Ama’s decision was corrected, but a 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science report still shows the earlier count.", "questions": "<strong>Questions:</strong> What do you verify? Can you simply edit the report total?", "answer": "<strong>Worked answer:</strong> Confirm the approved correction record, cohort filters, whether Search has been applied, and whether the underlying applicant/result record matches. Note time and any caching or API error evidence. Escalate the mismatch to the application owner; do not edit a report total as a substitute for correcting its source. The manual requires an approved policy for result correction. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a>", "search": "Scenario 3 · Result and report mismatch Fictional situation: An authorised Admissions officer says Ama’s decision was corrected, but a 2027 Semester 1 Computer Science report still shows the earlier count.Questions: What do you verify? Can you simply edit the report total?Worked answer: Confirm the approved correction record, cohort filters, whether Search has been applied, and whether the underlying applicant/result record matches. Note time and any caching or API error evidence. Escalate the mismatch to the application owner; do not edit a report total as a substitute for correcting its source. The manual requires an approved policy for result correction. M §10.11"}, {"id": "scenario4", "title": "Scenario 4 · First login fails", "situation": "<strong>Fictional situation:</strong> A positive admission result is visible to applicant Yaa Boateng, but first student login fails.", "questions": "<strong>Questions:</strong> Which boundary might have failed? What evidence do you send?", "answer": "<strong>Worked answer:</strong> Confirm the approved result, check whether the new-student creation step completed, then check identity/account state through authorised staff. Send the application reference, cohort, decision evidence, exact error and time—never a password. The manual specifically calls for first-login and duplicate-identity regression checks. <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a>", "search": "Scenario 4 · First login fails Fictional situation: A positive admission result is visible to applicant Yaa Boateng, but first student login fails.Questions: Which boundary might have failed? What evidence do you send?Worked answer: Confirm the approved result, check whether the new-student creation step completed, then check identity/account state through authorised staff. Send the application reference, cohort, decision evidence, exact error and time—never a password. The manual specifically calls for first-login and duplicate-identity regression checks. M §10.3"}], "meeting": [{"question": "What are the two staff work areas?", "answer": ""}, {"question": "Where does an applicant apply?", "answer": "<a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-01-11-product-surfaces\">M §1.1</a>"}, {"question": "Which filters identify one intake?", "answer": "<a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-03-31-terminology\">M §3.1</a> and UI figures."}, {"question": "Does “Pass” mean admitted?", "answer": ""}, {"question": "Who can change a result?", "answer": "<a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-1011-required-policy-register\">M §10.11</a>"}, {"question": "When is a student ID created?", "answer": "<a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UESD_CIS_Completion_Report_EN_Final_20260610.pdf#page=6\">P p. 6</a> <a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-10-103-admission-workflow\">M §10.3</a>"}, {"question": "What does first-line support do?", "answer": "<a class=\"ref\" href=\"sources/UEAS-Technical-Manual.html#en-06-65-investigating-a-production-issue\">M §6.5</a>"}]};
